1
00:00:00,430 --> 00:00:03,750
Rivers can grind walls of rock into dust.

2
00:00:03,800 --> 00:00:06,451
They carry millions of tonnes of soil

3
00:00:06,501 --> 00:00:08,941
and sand, and hold enough energy

4
00:00:08,991 --> 00:00:10,380
to power entire cities.

5
00:00:10,430 --> 00:00:12,140
There are long rivers,

6
00:00:12,190 --> 00:00:14,340
wide rivers and deep rivers.

7
00:00:14,390 --> 00:00:17,425
But by almost any measure, one river

8
00:00:17,475 --> 00:00:20,071
stands above them all: the Amazon.

9
00:00:20,821 --> 00:00:22,081
Its story begins high

10
00:00:22,131 --> 00:00:23,821
in the Andes Mountains,

11
00:00:23,871 --> 00:00:25,074
where water from glaciers

12
00:00:25,124 --> 00:00:27,831
and snowfields trickles down the slopes.

13
00:00:27,881 --> 00:00:28,878
Mountain streams such

14
00:00:28,928 --> 00:00:31,431
as the Urubamba River join together,

15
00:00:31,481 --> 00:00:33,556
forming the headwaters that will become

16
00:00:33,606 --> 00:00:35,302
the greatest river on Earth.

17
00:00:36,052 --> 00:00:38,182
As it crosses South America, the Amazon

18
00:00:38,232 --> 00:00:41,611
is fed by more than 1,100 tributaries,

19
00:00:41,661 --> 00:00:45,102
more than any other river in the world.

20
00:00:45,152 --> 00:00:47,922
Some arrive as thundering waterfalls,

21
00:00:47,972 --> 00:00:49,554
like those that carry water down

22
00:00:49,604 --> 00:00:51,373
from the Guiana Highlands.

23
00:00:52,123 --> 00:00:53,558
The Nile is usually

24
00:00:53,608 --> 00:00:55,393
considered slightly longer.

25
00:00:55,443 --> 00:00:56,764
But in volume,

26
00:00:56,814 --> 00:00:59,043
the Amazon leaves it far behind.

27
00:00:59,093 --> 00:01:02,393
Every second, the Nile pours about 5,100

28
00:01:02,443 --> 00:01:04,753
cubic metres of water into the sea.

29
00:01:04,803 --> 00:01:08,023
The Amazon pours about 220,000,

30
00:01:08,073 --> 00:01:09,429
around 43 times as much.

31
00:01:09,479 --> 00:01:12,791
In a single day, that is enough fresh water

32
00:01:12,841 --> 00:01:15,823
to supply a city for ten years.

33
00:01:15,873 --> 00:01:17,951
The next biggest rivers, the Congo,

34
00:01:18,001 --> 00:01:21,003
the Yangtze, the Yenisei and the Paraná,

35
00:01:21,053 --> 00:01:22,564
are not even close.

36
00:01:23,314 --> 00:01:24,694
In the wet season,

37
00:01:24,744 --> 00:01:26,684
the Amazon's floodwaters spread

38
00:01:26,734 --> 00:01:29,334
across an area about the size of Germany.

39
00:01:29,384 --> 00:01:31,988
Near its mouth, the river can be up

40
00:01:32,038 --> 00:01:33,814
to 40 kilometres wide.

41
00:01:33,864 --> 00:01:36,176
Its waters feed the largest rainforest

42
00:01:36,226 --> 00:01:37,056
on Earth, covering

43
00:01:37,106 --> 00:01:40,244
around five million square kilometres.

44
00:01:40,294 --> 00:01:42,039
Altogether, the Amazon holds

45
00:01:42,089 --> 00:01:44,761
about 20 percent of all the river water

46
00:01:44,811 --> 00:01:46,995
flowing into the world's oceans.

47
00:01:47,745 --> 00:01:49,861
Every year, seasonal rains flood

48
00:01:49,911 --> 00:01:52,841
the lowlands around the river, creating vast

49
00:01:52,891 --> 00:01:55,595
floodplains of forest standing in water.

50
00:01:55,645 --> 00:01:56,497
In these slow,

51
00:01:56,547 --> 00:01:59,205
muddy channels lives one of the Amazon's

52
00:01:59,255 --> 00:02:01,135
most mysterious animals:

53
00:02:01,185 --> 00:02:03,200
the river dolphin, which hunts fish

54
00:02:03,250 --> 00:02:05,565
and crabs in water so murky

55
00:02:05,615 --> 00:02:08,346
that scientists still know little about its life.

56
00:02:09,096 --> 00:02:11,946
The Amazon also carries a huge load:

57
00:02:11,996 --> 00:02:13,549
around three million cubic

58
00:02:13,599 --> 00:02:14,856
metres of sediment

59
00:02:14,906 --> 00:02:16,636
into the ocean every day.

60
00:02:16,686 --> 00:02:18,806
And where the river meets the sea,

61
00:02:18,856 --> 00:02:21,076
the incoming tide pushes back.

62
00:02:21,126 --> 00:02:23,498
Long waves called tidal bores,

63
00:02:23,548 --> 00:02:25,986
or pororocas, roll upstream,

64
00:02:26,036 --> 00:02:28,509
and surfers can ride a single wave for up

65
00:02:28,559 --> 00:02:29,817
to ten kilometres.

66
00:02:31,667 --> 00:02:33,297
On our restless planet,

67
00:02:33,347 --> 00:02:35,447
nothing stays the same for long.

68
00:02:35,497 --> 00:02:38,027
Landscapes are always being carved,

69
00:02:38,077 --> 00:02:40,047
worn down and reshaped.

70
00:02:40,097 --> 00:02:42,053
A human life is far too short

71
00:02:42,103 --> 00:02:43,427
to watch it happen.

72
00:02:43,477 --> 00:02:45,948
But the results are all around us.

73
00:02:46,698 --> 00:02:50,128
In dry places, the wind becomes a sculptor.

74
00:02:50,178 --> 00:02:52,581
It lifts grains of sand and hurls them

75
00:02:52,631 --> 00:02:55,228
against rock like a sandblaster.

76
00:02:55,278 --> 00:02:58,002
In Arches National Park in Utah, this slow

77
00:02:58,052 --> 00:03:01,378
attack has carved natural stone arches,

78
00:03:01,428 --> 00:03:04,068
balancing rocks and flat-topped mesas

79
00:03:04,118 --> 00:03:06,499
that stand like monuments in the desert.

80
00:03:07,249 --> 00:03:09,010
Sometimes the wind leaves

81
00:03:09,060 --> 00:03:10,819
strange shapes behind.

82
00:03:10,869 --> 00:03:12,850
Hoodoos are tall spires of rock

83
00:03:12,900 --> 00:03:15,989
with a cap of harder stone on top,

84
00:03:16,039 --> 00:03:18,279
protecting the softer rock beneath.

85
00:03:18,329 --> 00:03:20,167
In Australia, the Devils Marbles

86
00:03:20,217 --> 00:03:22,729
are giant round boulders,

87
00:03:22,779 --> 00:03:24,659
sandblasted over time until they seem

88
00:03:24,709 --> 00:03:27,050
to balance on top of one another.

89
00:03:27,800 --> 00:03:30,650
Water can be an even stronger carver.

90
00:03:30,700 --> 00:03:32,533
When fast-moving water rushes

91
00:03:32,583 --> 00:03:35,433
through a crack in soft rock, it can cut

92
00:03:35,483 --> 00:03:37,970
a deep, narrow channel with smooth,

93
00:03:38,020 --> 00:03:40,651
curving walls: a slot canyon, glowing

94
00:03:40,701 --> 00:03:43,841
with light that filters down from far above.

95
00:03:44,591 --> 00:03:46,815
The greatest rock carver of all

96
00:03:46,865 --> 00:03:48,121
is the Colorado River.

97
00:03:48,171 --> 00:03:51,591
Over millions of years, it has cut

98
00:03:51,641 --> 00:03:54,301
the Grand Canyon: 446 kilometres long,

99
00:03:54,351 --> 00:03:56,575
up to 29 kilometres wide, and in places

100
00:03:56,625 --> 00:03:59,741
more than 1.6 kilometres deep, exposing

101
00:03:59,791 --> 00:04:02,152
rocks close to two billion years old.

102
00:04:02,202 --> 00:04:05,641
Its power comes from its steep slope,

103
00:04:05,691 --> 00:04:06,822
the huge amount of mud,

104
00:04:06,872 --> 00:04:08,536
sand and gravel it carries,

105
00:04:08,586 --> 00:04:11,911
and the relatively soft rock it flows through.

106
00:04:12,661 --> 00:04:15,641
Along the coast, the ocean never rests.

107
00:04:15,691 --> 00:04:17,772
Waves crash against the rocks,

108
00:04:17,821 --> 00:04:18,928
armed with sand and grit

109
00:04:18,978 --> 00:04:21,131
that grind the cliffs away.

110
00:04:21,181 --> 00:04:24,341
Over time, the base of a cliff is undercut

111
00:04:24,391 --> 00:04:26,991
until the rock above collapses into the sea.

112
00:04:27,041 --> 00:04:29,591
In Australia, the Painted Cliffs were carved

113
00:04:29,641 --> 00:04:32,104
this way, their layers stained orange

114
00:04:32,154 --> 00:04:33,462
and red by minerals.

115
00:04:35,312 --> 00:04:37,402
In the coldest places on Earth,

116
00:04:37,452 --> 00:04:39,802
snow falls but rarely melts.

117
00:04:39,852 --> 00:04:42,232
Layer upon layer piles up,

118
00:04:42,282 --> 00:04:45,187
and the weight squeezes the fluffy snow

119
00:04:45,237 --> 00:04:46,832
into dense, solid ice.

120
00:04:46,882 --> 00:04:49,742
Slowly, under the pull of gravity,

121
00:04:49,792 --> 00:04:52,522
this ice begins to move downhill.

122
00:04:52,572 --> 00:04:55,972
These giant rivers of ice are glaciers,

123
00:04:56,022 --> 00:04:57,152
and as they creep,

124
00:04:57,202 --> 00:04:59,673
they scour a path through the land.

125
00:05:00,423 --> 00:05:03,293
At the front of a glacier, the ice can

126
00:05:03,343 --> 00:05:05,523
break away with a thunderous crack.

127
00:05:05,573 --> 00:05:08,979
In Argentina, the Perito Moreno Glacier

128
00:05:09,029 --> 00:05:11,133
pushes into Lago Argentino,

129
00:05:11,183 --> 00:05:13,083
the country's largest lake.

130
00:05:13,133 --> 00:05:14,971
Huge towers of ice crash

131
00:05:15,021 --> 00:05:17,203
from its wall into the water,

132
00:05:17,253 --> 00:05:19,504
sending waves across the lake.

133
00:05:20,254 --> 00:05:23,394
A glacier is always melting and freezing,

134
00:05:23,444 --> 00:05:25,294
changing with the time of day,

135
00:05:25,344 --> 00:05:27,274
the season and the climate.

136
00:05:27,324 --> 00:05:29,724
When more ice forms than melts,

137
00:05:29,774 --> 00:05:32,024
the glacier grows and advances.

138
00:05:32,074 --> 00:05:34,934
When more melts, it shrinks and retreats.

139
00:05:34,984 --> 00:05:37,605
In Alaska, the enormous Hubbard Glacier

140
00:05:37,655 --> 00:05:38,910
has been advancing

141
00:05:38,960 --> 00:05:41,054
for more than a hundred years.

142
00:05:41,104 --> 00:05:42,898
Cruise ships that sail up

143
00:05:42,948 --> 00:05:44,855
to its face look like toys.

144
00:05:45,605 --> 00:05:48,326
Most glaciers move only a few centimetres

145
00:05:48,376 --> 00:05:49,495
or metres a day.

146
00:05:49,545 --> 00:05:51,835
But some are surprisingly fast.

147
00:05:51,885 --> 00:05:52,990
The Jakobshavn Glacier

148
00:05:53,040 --> 00:05:55,985
in Greenland is one of the fastest on Earth,

149
00:05:56,035 --> 00:05:58,126
flowing at up to 20 metres a day

150
00:05:58,176 --> 00:06:00,086
as it pours ice into the sea.

151
00:06:00,836 --> 00:06:03,896
When glaciers and ice sheets reach

152
00:06:03,946 --> 00:06:05,762
the ocean, great chunks break off

153
00:06:05,812 --> 00:06:07,506
and float away as icebergs.

154
00:06:07,556 --> 00:06:09,986
Because ice is less dense than water,

155
00:06:10,036 --> 00:06:13,746
about 90 percent of an iceberg hides

156
00:06:13,796 --> 00:06:14,682
below the surface.

157
00:06:14,732 --> 00:06:16,159
That hidden danger has

158
00:06:16,209 --> 00:06:17,942
always threatened ships.

159
00:06:17,992 --> 00:06:20,690
In 1912, an iceberg sank the Titanic,

160
00:06:20,740 --> 00:06:23,957
and more than 1,500 people lost their lives.

161
00:06:24,707 --> 00:06:25,695
For some animals,

162
00:06:25,745 --> 00:06:27,687
though, icebergs are useful.

163
00:06:27,737 --> 00:06:28,748
In the Southern Ocean,

164
00:06:28,798 --> 00:06:30,702
colonies of penguins hitch rides

165
00:06:30,752 --> 00:06:33,083
on floating ice, resting and travelling

166
00:06:33,133 --> 00:06:35,220
until hunger sends them diving back

167
00:06:35,270 --> 00:06:36,868
into the sea to catch fish.

168
00:06:37,618 --> 00:06:40,128
Earth's climate has always changed, and ice

169
00:06:40,178 --> 00:06:43,058
ages are a normal part of its history.

170
00:06:43,108 --> 00:06:44,938
During the last ice age,

171
00:06:44,988 --> 00:06:47,158
so much water was locked up in ice

172
00:06:47,208 --> 00:06:50,718
that sea levels fell by around 120 metres.

173
00:06:50,768 --> 00:06:52,436
Woolly mammoths grazed on cold

174
00:06:52,486 --> 00:06:55,958
grasslands, hunted by sabre-toothed cats.

175
00:06:56,008 --> 00:06:57,579
Today, the opposite is happening

176
00:06:57,629 --> 00:06:59,048
in many places.

177
00:06:59,098 --> 00:07:00,891
The ice cap on Mount Kilimanjaro

178
00:07:00,941 --> 00:07:03,338
in Africa is melting so fast that scientists

179
00:07:03,388 --> 00:07:06,739
warn it could disappear within decades.

180
00:07:08,589 --> 00:07:11,689
Deep beneath our feet lies a hidden world.

181
00:07:11,739 --> 00:07:13,966
Dark caverns and winding tunnels stretch

182
00:07:14,016 --> 00:07:16,679
far into the Earth, their walls decorated

183
00:07:16,729 --> 00:07:19,179
with strange stone shapes.

184
00:07:19,229 --> 00:07:21,599
For the brave explorers who venture down,

185
00:07:21,649 --> 00:07:22,900
the rewards are some of the

186
00:07:22,950 --> 00:07:25,150
most awesome sights on the planet.

187
00:07:25,900 --> 00:07:28,360
Most caves are carved from limestone,

188
00:07:28,410 --> 00:07:31,080
and limestone has an extraordinary origin.

189
00:07:31,130 --> 00:07:33,590
Its main ingredient, calcium carbonate,

190
00:07:33,640 --> 00:07:34,502
comes from the shells

191
00:07:34,552 --> 00:07:36,850
and skeletons of ancient sea creatures.

192
00:07:36,900 --> 00:07:39,189
Over millions of years, they piled up

193
00:07:39,239 --> 00:07:40,920
on the seafloor and were pressed,

194
00:07:40,970 --> 00:07:43,221
layer by layer, into solid rock.

195
00:07:43,971 --> 00:07:46,151
When rainwater soaks through the ground,

196
00:07:46,201 --> 00:07:48,208
it picks up carbon dioxide

197
00:07:48,258 --> 00:07:49,771
and becomes slightly acidic.

198
00:07:49,821 --> 00:07:52,881
This weak acid slowly dissolves limestone,

199
00:07:52,931 --> 00:07:55,051
hollowing out tunnels and chambers.

200
00:07:55,101 --> 00:07:56,351
Inside the cave,

201
00:07:56,401 --> 00:07:58,921
dripping water leaves its minerals behind.

202
00:07:58,971 --> 00:08:00,158
From the ceiling grow

203
00:08:00,208 --> 00:08:01,991
icicle-shaped stalactites.

204
00:08:02,041 --> 00:08:02,941
From the floor,

205
00:08:02,991 --> 00:08:04,961
stalagmites rise to meet them,

206
00:08:05,011 --> 00:08:07,692
often just a fraction of a millimetre a year.

207
00:08:08,442 --> 00:08:10,199
Sometimes, the ground above

208
00:08:10,249 --> 00:08:12,082
a hollow simply gives way.

209
00:08:12,132 --> 00:08:13,812
When weakened rock collapses

210
00:08:13,862 --> 00:08:16,572
into the cavern below, a sinkhole opens,

211
00:08:16,622 --> 00:08:18,002
often without warning.

212
00:08:18,052 --> 00:08:20,562
In Guatemala, during a tropical storm,

213
00:08:20,612 --> 00:08:22,922
a sinkhole about 30 storeys deep suddenly

214
00:08:22,972 --> 00:08:25,523
swallowed a three-storey building.

215
00:08:26,273 --> 00:08:29,543
Caves are among the least explored places

216
00:08:29,593 --> 00:08:31,983
on Earth, which is exactly why cave

217
00:08:32,033 --> 00:08:34,118
explorers, called spelunkers or potholers,

218
00:08:34,168 --> 00:08:35,863
find them so thrilling.

219
00:08:35,913 --> 00:08:38,893
But every expedition carries real risks:

220
00:08:38,943 --> 00:08:42,263
collapsing rock, freezing water, falls,

221
00:08:42,313 --> 00:08:45,394
sudden floods, and getting lost in the dark.

222
00:08:46,144 --> 00:08:48,393
In Gunung Mulu National Park

223
00:08:48,443 --> 00:08:50,104
on the island of Borneo,

224
00:08:50,154 --> 00:08:52,334
cave experts have mapped

225
00:08:52,384 --> 00:08:54,944
more than 320 kilometres of passages.

226
00:08:54,994 --> 00:08:57,474
Here lies the Sarawak Chamber,

227
00:08:57,524 --> 00:09:00,264
the largest known cave chamber by area:

228
00:09:00,314 --> 00:09:02,284
about 700 metres long

229
00:09:02,334 --> 00:09:04,124
and 100 metres high.

230
00:09:04,174 --> 00:09:05,274
Nearby, Deer Cave

231
00:09:05,324 --> 00:09:08,100
was discovered only in 1976.

232
00:09:08,150 --> 00:09:11,467
And in Vietnam, Hang Sơn Đoòng is so vast

233
00:09:11,517 --> 00:09:14,714
it has its own jungle and clouds.

234
00:09:14,764 --> 00:09:17,625
It is the largest cave passage ever found.

235
00:09:18,375 --> 00:09:20,167
The longest cave system

236
00:09:20,217 --> 00:09:23,055
on Earth is Mammoth Cave, in Kentucky.

237
00:09:23,105 --> 00:09:25,211
Explorers have now mapped

238
00:09:25,261 --> 00:09:28,845
more than 686 kilometres of its passages,

239
00:09:28,895 --> 00:09:31,056
and they are still finding more.

240
00:09:32,906 --> 00:09:35,586
Earth's crust is a treasure chest.

241
00:09:35,636 --> 00:09:37,746
Many things we use every day,

242
00:09:37,796 --> 00:09:39,123
from soft talcum powder

243
00:09:39,173 --> 00:09:41,606
to the graphite "lead" in a pencil,

244
00:09:41,656 --> 00:09:43,876
were formed deep inside it.

245
00:09:43,926 --> 00:09:46,416
Getting them out takes enormous effort,

246
00:09:46,466 --> 00:09:49,107
human courage and clever technology.

247
00:09:49,857 --> 00:09:51,657
Our buildings are made of it.

248
00:09:51,707 --> 00:09:54,657
Marble is strong and easy to carve, and has

249
00:09:54,707 --> 00:09:58,587
been used for some of the most famous

250
00:09:58,637 --> 00:09:59,685
buildings in history.

251
00:09:59,735 --> 00:10:02,327
Iron, mixed with other elements,

252
00:10:02,377 --> 00:10:03,059
becomes steel.

253
00:10:03,109 --> 00:10:05,067
Aluminium is light and strong.

254
00:10:05,117 --> 00:10:07,888
Gypsum, when heated, becomes plaster

255
00:10:07,938 --> 00:10:10,578
for walls and chalk for blackboards.

256
00:10:11,328 --> 00:10:13,558
Our machines run on it.

257
00:10:13,608 --> 00:10:15,901
Copper bends easily and carries electricity

258
00:10:15,951 --> 00:10:19,508
better than almost any other metal.

259
00:10:19,558 --> 00:10:22,768
Nickel goes into coins and batteries.

260
00:10:22,818 --> 00:10:24,021
Mercury, the only metal

261
00:10:24,071 --> 00:10:26,868
that is liquid at room temperature,

262
00:10:26,918 --> 00:10:29,569
was used for centuries in thermometers.

263
00:10:30,319 --> 00:10:31,052
And the phone

264
00:10:31,102 --> 00:10:32,839
in your pocket depends on it too.

265
00:10:32,889 --> 00:10:34,907
A mineral called coltan is used

266
00:10:34,957 --> 00:10:36,229
to make mobile phones.

267
00:10:36,279 --> 00:10:37,738
But much of it comes from

268
00:10:37,788 --> 00:10:39,359
the Democratic Republic of the Congo,

269
00:10:39,409 --> 00:10:42,479
where miners often dig by hand

270
00:10:42,529 --> 00:10:44,123
in dangerous ground, risking mudslides,

271
00:10:44,173 --> 00:10:45,979
toxic fumes and collapsing pits,

272
00:10:46,029 --> 00:10:48,280
all in conditions of extreme poverty.

273
00:10:49,030 --> 00:10:51,430
Some minerals are simply beautiful.

274
00:10:51,480 --> 00:10:53,808
Fluorite glows with intense colour

275
00:10:53,858 --> 00:10:55,630
under ultraviolet light.

276
00:10:55,680 --> 00:10:58,030
Hematite, ground into powder, became

277
00:10:58,080 --> 00:11:00,960
the red paint used in ancient rock art.

278
00:11:01,010 --> 00:11:02,974
And gold can be hammered thinner

279
00:11:03,024 --> 00:11:05,702
than any other metal: a single gram can

280
00:11:05,752 --> 00:11:07,650
be beaten into a sheet of gold leaf

281
00:11:07,700 --> 00:11:09,551
a square metre wide.

282
00:11:10,301 --> 00:11:13,161
The most precious of all is diamond.

283
00:11:13,211 --> 00:11:16,101
Amazingly, it is made of pure carbon,

284
00:11:16,151 --> 00:11:16,913
the same element

285
00:11:16,963 --> 00:11:19,501
as the soft graphite in your pencil.

286
00:11:19,551 --> 00:11:21,925
Arranged differently, carbon becomes

287
00:11:21,975 --> 00:11:24,441
the hardest natural mineral on Earth.

288
00:11:24,491 --> 00:11:26,651
The Imperial Crown of India holds

289
00:11:26,701 --> 00:11:29,171
more than 6,000 diamonds.

290
00:11:29,221 --> 00:11:31,721
Other elements are far less stable.

291
00:11:31,771 --> 00:11:33,091
Potassium is so reactive

292
00:11:33,141 --> 00:11:36,121
that it is never found pure in nature,

293
00:11:36,171 --> 00:11:37,586
and it bursts into flame

294
00:11:37,636 --> 00:11:39,182
on contact with water.

295
00:11:39,932 --> 00:11:42,417
Reaching Earth's buried treasure can

296
00:11:42,467 --> 00:11:44,092
be extremely dangerous.

297
00:11:44,142 --> 00:11:46,342
On 20 April 2010, the Deepwater Horizon

298
00:11:46,392 --> 00:11:49,529
oil rig in the Gulf of Mexico exploded.

299
00:11:49,579 --> 00:11:51,431
Eleven workers were killed.

300
00:11:51,481 --> 00:11:52,663
The rig had been drilling

301
00:11:52,713 --> 00:11:56,522
beneath about 1,500 metres of water,

302
00:11:56,572 --> 00:11:58,000
a task some engineers compared

303
00:11:58,050 --> 00:11:59,852
to exploring the Moon.

304
00:11:59,902 --> 00:12:02,275
Around five million barrels of oil poured

305
00:12:02,325 --> 00:12:03,442
into the sea, causing

306
00:12:03,492 --> 00:12:06,853
the largest accidental oil spill in history.

307
00:12:08,703 --> 00:12:11,556
The deep ocean is the least explored place

308
00:12:11,606 --> 00:12:12,563
on our planet.

309
00:12:12,613 --> 00:12:15,123
It is pitch-dark, freezing cold,

310
00:12:15,173 --> 00:12:17,433
and the pressure could crush a submarine.

311
00:12:17,483 --> 00:12:20,293
Yet even here, life finds a way,

312
00:12:20,343 --> 00:12:22,269
from colossal squid with eyes bigger

313
00:12:22,319 --> 00:12:23,733
than a human head,

314
00:12:23,783 --> 00:12:25,864
to fish that make their own light.

315
00:12:26,614 --> 00:12:29,444
As we dive, the ocean changes.

316
00:12:29,494 --> 00:12:31,704
In the sunlit zone near the surface,

317
00:12:31,754 --> 00:12:33,864
there is enough light for plants to grow.

318
00:12:33,914 --> 00:12:37,514
Below about 200 metres begins the twilight

319
00:12:37,564 --> 00:12:39,924
zone, where only a faint glow remains.

320
00:12:39,974 --> 00:12:42,914
Past 1,000 metres is the dark zone.

321
00:12:42,964 --> 00:12:45,944
Below 4,000 metres lies the abyssal zone.

322
00:12:45,994 --> 00:12:47,463
And deeper than 6,000 metres,

323
00:12:47,513 --> 00:12:50,754
in the ocean trenches, is the hadal zone,

324
00:12:50,804 --> 00:12:52,044
one of the most extreme

325
00:12:52,094 --> 00:12:54,335
and least known places on Earth.

326
00:12:55,085 --> 00:12:57,725
In the darkness, it snows.

327
00:12:57,775 --> 00:13:00,645
Tiny bits of dead plants and animals,

328
00:13:00,695 --> 00:13:02,335
called marine snow,

329
00:13:02,385 --> 00:13:04,835
drift down from the waters above.

330
00:13:04,885 --> 00:13:06,915
Over thousands of years,

331
00:13:06,965 --> 00:13:08,655
they settle on the seabed

332
00:13:08,705 --> 00:13:12,865
and build up layers of fine mud and ooze,

333
00:13:12,915 --> 00:13:15,565
some of them up to 450 metres thick.

334
00:13:16,315 --> 00:13:18,327
The creatures of the deep look

335
00:13:18,377 --> 00:13:20,005
like they come from another world.

336
00:13:20,055 --> 00:13:23,207
The Dumbo octopus flaps ear-like fins

337
00:13:23,257 --> 00:13:24,765
as it hovers above the seafloor,

338
00:13:24,815 --> 00:13:26,715
thousands of metres down.

339
00:13:26,765 --> 00:13:30,275
Giant ostracods, relatives of tiny shrimp,

340
00:13:30,325 --> 00:13:31,936
glide through the dark water

341
00:13:31,986 --> 00:13:33,516
like floating lanterns.

342
00:13:34,266 --> 00:13:35,603
On the ocean floor,

343
00:13:35,653 --> 00:13:38,416
cracks in the crust release volcanic heat.

344
00:13:38,466 --> 00:13:40,490
These hydrothermal vents,

345
00:13:40,540 --> 00:13:42,387
also called black smokers,

346
00:13:42,437 --> 00:13:44,576
gush mineral-rich water as hot

347
00:13:44,626 --> 00:13:46,416
as 400 degrees Celsius.

348
00:13:46,466 --> 00:13:47,846
Instead of sunlight,

349
00:13:47,896 --> 00:13:49,826
life here runs on chemicals.

350
00:13:49,876 --> 00:13:51,434
Blind shrimp, starfish,

351
00:13:51,484 --> 00:13:53,330
tube worms and fish gather

352
00:13:53,380 --> 00:13:56,467
around the vents like an oasis in a desert.

353
00:13:57,217 --> 00:13:59,284
The deepest point of any ocean

354
00:13:59,334 --> 00:14:01,087
is the Challenger Deep,

355
00:14:01,137 --> 00:14:03,557
in the Mariana Trench in the Pacific.

356
00:14:03,607 --> 00:14:05,037
In 1960, two explorers,

357
00:14:05,087 --> 00:14:08,287
Jacques Piccard and Don Walsh, climbed

358
00:14:08,337 --> 00:14:10,115
into a submersible called the Trieste

359
00:14:10,165 --> 00:14:12,479
and sank nearly 11 kilometres

360
00:14:12,529 --> 00:14:13,467
to the bottom.

361
00:14:13,517 --> 00:14:15,725
It was one of the most dangerous

362
00:14:15,775 --> 00:14:17,418
journeys ever attempted.

363
00:14:18,168 --> 00:14:20,024
For more than fifty years,

364
00:14:20,074 --> 00:14:21,518
they were almost alone.

365
00:14:21,568 --> 00:14:23,763
Filmmaker James Cameron made

366
00:14:23,813 --> 00:14:25,418
a solo dive in 2012,

367
00:14:25,468 --> 00:14:27,868
and since 2019 a new submersible has

368
00:14:27,918 --> 00:14:30,348
carried more explorers down.

369
00:14:30,398 --> 00:14:32,120
By 2022, 27 people had reached

370
00:14:32,170 --> 00:14:33,586
the Challenger Deep.

371
00:14:33,636 --> 00:14:36,811
That is still only a little more than the 12

372
00:14:36,861 --> 00:14:38,898
people who have walked on the Moon,

373
00:14:38,948 --> 00:14:41,649
nearly 400,000 kilometres away.

374
00:14:42,399 --> 00:14:43,488
Because the deep sea

375
00:14:43,538 --> 00:14:45,189
is so dangerous for humans,

376
00:14:45,239 --> 00:14:47,569
scientists now explore it with robots.

377
00:14:47,619 --> 00:14:49,352
Remotely operated vehicles are steered

378
00:14:49,402 --> 00:14:51,069
from ships on the surface,

379
00:14:51,119 --> 00:14:52,767
while autonomous vehicles navigate

380
00:14:52,817 --> 00:14:54,209
the seabed on their own.

381
00:14:54,259 --> 00:14:55,898
With sonar, they map underwater

382
00:14:55,948 --> 00:14:58,176
mountains and valleys we have never seen

383
00:14:58,226 --> 00:14:59,200
with our own eyes.
